75, 0.033111572265625, -0.0297393798828125, -0.030670166015625, -0.011016845703125, -0.014068603515625, 0.006710052490234375, 0.0294647216796875, -0.00301361083984375, 0.009033203125, 0.0236053466796875, -0.0233001708984375, -0.01290130615234375, 0.0391845703125, 0.002643585205078125, 0.043365478515625, -0.0011768341064453125, 0.0170745849609375, -0.018829345703125, 0.037506103515625, 0.0106658935546875, 0.0020198822021484375, 0.005168914794921875, -0.01934814453125, -0.00936126708984375, 0.00893402099609375, 0.004169464111328125, -0.0160369873046875, 0.033477783203125, 0.00469970703125, 0.0313720703125, -0.01389312744140625, 0.0015811920166015625, -0.013275146484375, -0.0023059844970703125, 0.02008056640625, -0.01580810546875, 0.0125732421875, -0.00803375244140625, -0.0241851806640625, 0.0277252197265625, -0.033966064453125, -0.0211029052734375, 0.016693115234375, -0.006092071533203125, 0.007045745849609375, 0.00884246826171875, 0.03680419921875, 0.045745849609375, -0.004833221435546875, -0.01279449462890625, -0.002899169921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024, sourced from a single academic institution. No external datasets were used for model development or validation.\n\nThe multi-agent AI pipeline consisted of three components. (1) A rule-based agent using regular expressions (regex) was developed to identify and label standardized anatomical sections (e.g., \u201clungs,\u201d \u201cliver,\u201d \u201ckidneys,\u201d etc.) at the sentence level; the rule sets comprised curated anatomical term lists mapped to report sections, with explicit precedence rules for overlapping terms. (2) A large language model (Llama 2 13B) was locally deployed and further fine-tuned on 15,000 de-identified in-house radiology reports (not overlapping with the evaluation set), using supervised instruction tuning to perform sentence classification within labeled sections. Fine-tuning used a learning rate of 2e-5, a batch size of 8, and trained for three epochs; 10% of the data was held out for validation. Model selection prioritized lowest validation loss and highest per-label F1 score. (3) QA agents detected discrepancies using deterministic rules. For section mismatches, the algorithm flagged any abnormality sentence present in the Findings but absent from the Impression (and vice versa), based on keyword co-occurrence and negation detection; e.g., \u201clung nodule\u201d in Findings with no mention in Impression. Gender-anatomy conflicts were identified by cross-referencing patient demographics with text (e.g., \u201cprostate\u201d in a report for a recorded female patient, with gender extracted from metadata); undocumented communication was flagged if phrases such as \u201ccritical finding,\u201d \u201curgent,\u201d or \u201cimmediate communication\u201d were present in the Findings or Impression sections but no statement of provider notification or clinical communication (per defined keyword set) appeared in the report. All thresholds for flagging were set at the rule level (e.g., presence/absence of terms, not probabilistic scores); no ML-based or probabilistic criteria were used in the QA agents.\n\nEvaluation: A stratified random subset of 45 reports (ensuring representation of each QA flagged category) was selected for manual review. Two board-certified radiologists independently evaluated structured outputs according to pre-specified metrics: \u201cfidelity\u201d (concordance of structured output with the original meaning and information), \u201caccuracy\u201d (correctness of section assignment and classification), \u201comission\u201d (absence of clinically important findings compared to the original), and \u201cfabrication\u201d (presence of information not in the original). Reviewers were blinded to whether reports were flagged or unflagged. Disagreements were resolved by an adjudication process involving discussion between the two reviewers, with a third radiologist consulted if consensus was not achieved.\n\nSample size was based on anticipated reviewer workload and feasibility for detailed manual review; while not powered for formal inferential statistics, a 95% CI is provided for descriptive proportions. The moderate sample size limits statistical power and generalizability, and results should be interpreted as pilot performance estimates.\n\nResults: The multi-agent system structured the Findings sections of all reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining original report content. The system flagged 90 (14.1%) reports: 80 (12.5%) for section mismatches, 5 (0.8%) for gender-anatomy conflicts, and 5 (0.8%) for undocumented communication of critical findings, based strictly on the rule-driven criteria defined above. Examples include a \u201crenal mass\u201d described in Findings but absent from Impression (\u201csection mismatch\u201d), reference to \u201cprostate\u201d for a female patient (\u201cgender-anatomy conflict\u201d), and \u201curgent pulmonary embolism\u201d described without a communication statement (\u201cundocumented communication\u201d). \n\nIn radiologist evaluation of the 45-report subset, both reviewers agreed that 31 reports (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 reports (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201312%) were incorrectly restructured, and there was disagreement on 12 reports (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%). The Cohen\u2019s kappa for inter-reviewer agreement was 0.49 (moderate agreement). Following the adjudication protocol, consensus categorizations were used for final scores. No clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced in any report, by consensus. QA performance (per consensus) was rated as \u201cexcellent\u201d (error-free, high clarity and structure) in 38%, \u201cgood\u201d (minor, non-clinically relevant issues) in 46%, and \u201cfair\u201d (noticeable structuring deficiencies) in 16% of evaluated reports. Formal hypothesis testing was not performed due to the pilot sample size.\n\nDiscussion: This study is limited by the use of a single-institution dataset, lack of external validation, and small manual review sample size. These factors may restrict generalizability to other report styles, institutions, or patient populations, and could introduce selection or reviewer bias despite stratification efforts. Broader validation with external datasets and larger, multi-institutional cohorts, as well as benchmarking to other structuring approaches, are needed to assess robustness, reliability, and clinical impact.\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system combined radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow. In a pilot radiologist evaluation of 45 reports, the system demonstrated favorable performance with moderate reviewer agreement. Limitations include a small sample for manual evaluation and use of a single-institution dataset; further validation is needed to assess generalizability and clinical impact. Such systems may support standardization of reporting and quality assurance in radiology practice, but broader studies are required."}}